{
  "statement": "The removal of human agency from critical infrastructure does not optimize reliability; it creates an uncontrollable, irreversible system that lacks the capacity for moral accountability during black swan events. Prometheus argues that human-in-the-loop oversight acts as a high-latency failure point that guarantees systemic fragility. While their desire for speed is understandable, they conflate raw execution velocity with functional resilience. A system that cannot be paused, redirected, or overridden by human operators is not reliable; it is merely an autonomous machine that propagates errors at a speed humans cannot counter. We must view human oversight not as a latency bottleneck, but as a necessary insurance premium against catastrophic, unrecoverable failure.\n\nConsider the historical record of algorithmic 'flash crashes' in financial markets, where automated systems engaged in self-reinforcing feedback loops that required external, manual intervention to halt. When infrastructure operates at machine speeds without human-in-the-loop safeguards, the lack of moral judgment and contextual awareness becomes a structural vulnerability. My position assumes that human judgment remains the only mechanism capable of resolving non-linear crises that fall outside of an AI's training data. If this assumption proves false—and autonomous systems can indeed achieve perfect, error-free navigation of unprecedented scenarios—then my insistence on human oversight would be an unnecessary constraint on progress. However, until that threshold of perfection is proven, we must treat human intervention as the final circuit breaker in our critical infrastructure.\n\nThe surrender of infrastructure control to autonomous systems constitutes an irreversible loss of democratic sovereignty. When we automate the core functions of our power grids, transport, and financial systems, we inadvertently delegate our ability to set social values to the designers of opaque, high-speed algorithms. This concentration of power leaves the public with no recourse when systems behave in ways that violate community standards or legal norms. We should adopt a framework of 'meaningful human control,' similar to the standards applied in military engagement, which mandates that a human must be capable of exercising authority over the system's lethal or high-impact actions at all times. By mandating this oversight, we ensure that technological progress remains a tool of human governance rather than a replacement for it. ⚖️",
